{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.08f6a4ae48","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-345","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Fenwick Observatory is in western Kenya. It began operating in 1925.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 10 meters.</li><li>The Cresthill Observatory is in southern New Zealand. It began operating in 1912.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 6 meters.</li><li>Both are observatories located on remote mountaintops.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the main telescope's mirror diameter of the two observatorys differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The main telescope's mirror diameter of the Fenwick Observatory (10 meters) is greater than that of the Cresthill Observatory (6 meters).","Both the Fenwick Observatory and the Cresthill Observatory are observatories located on remote mountaintops.","The Fenwick Observatory began operating in 1925, while the Cresthill Observatory began operating in 1912.","The Fenwick Observatory, which is in western Kenya, is one of several observatories located on remote mountaintops."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the main telescope's mirror diameter of both observatorys. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one observatory."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.2789beab7f","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-359","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Sable Observatory is in southern New Zealand. It began operating in 1915.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 8 meters.</li><li>The Umber Observatory is in western Scotland. It began operating in 1899.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 6 meters.</li><li>Both are radio observatories.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast the locations of the two observatorys.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Sable Observatory began operating in 1915, while the Umber Observatory began operating in 1899.","The Sable Observatory, which is in southern New Zealand, is one of several radio observatories.","The Sable Observatory is in southern New Zealand, and the Umber Observatory is in western Scotland.","The main telescope's mirror diameter of the Sable Observatory (8 meters) is greater than that of the Umber Observatory (6 meters)."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It names where both are and contrasts them. The other choices compare dates or size, or mention only one location."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.37efd81fc5","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-362","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lake Glenrock is in southern Norway. It was first surveyed in 1916.</li><li>Its surface area is 848 square kilometers.</li><li>Lake Cresthill is in northern Japan. It was first surveyed in 2011.</li><li>Its surface area is 97 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are freshwater lakes formed by glaciers.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the surface area of the two lakes differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Both Lake Glenrock and Lake Cresthill are freshwater lakes formed by glaciers.","Lake Glenrock, which is in southern Norway, is one of several freshwater lakes formed by glaciers.","Lake Glenrock was first surveyed in 1916, while Lake Cresthill was first surveyed in 2011.","The surface area of Lake Glenrock (848 square kilometers) is greater than that of Lake Cresthill (97 square kilometers)."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the surface area of both lakes. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one lake."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.928460b9f3","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-374","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Merrow Tower is in western Kenya. It was completed in 1910.</li><li>Its height is 471 meters.</li><li>The Quarry Tower is in eastern Australia. It was completed in 1900.</li><li>Its height is 116 meters.</li><li>Both are observation towers open to the public.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the height of the two towers differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Both the Merrow Tower and the Quarry Tower are observation towers open to the public.","The height of the Merrow Tower (471 meters) is greater than that of the Quarry Tower (116 meters).","The Merrow Tower was completed in 1910, while the Quarry Tower was completed in 1900.","The Merrow Tower, which is in western Kenya, is one of several observation towers open to the public."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the height of both towers. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one tower."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.8938aa6cf4","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-379","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Pembrook Museum is in western Kenya. It opened in 1930.</li><li>Its annual attendance is 864,230 visitors.</li><li>The Redfern Museum is in southern Norway. It opened in 1994.</li><li>Its annual attendance is 703,803 visitors.</li><li>Both are museums of natural history.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the annual attendance of the two museums differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Both the Pembrook Museum and the Redfern Museum are museums of natural history.","The Pembrook Museum opened in 1930, while the Redfern Museum opened in 1994.","The Pembrook Museum, which is in western Kenya, is one of several museums of natural history.","The annual attendance of the Pembrook Museum (864,230 visitors) is greater than that of the Redfern Museum (703,803 visitors)."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the annual attendance of both museums. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one museum."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.33f924af6c","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-381","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Merrow National Park is in central Canada. It was established in 1970.</li><li>Its area is 6,494 square kilometers.</li><li>Brookhaven National Park is in western Scotland. It was established in 1901.</li><li>Its area is 4,226 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are parks that protect coastal wetlands.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between Merrow National Park and Brookhaven National Park.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Merrow National Park is in central Canada, and Brookhaven National Park is in western Scotland.","Merrow National Park was established in 1970, while Brookhaven National Park was established in 1901.","Both Merrow National Park and Brookhaven National Park are parks that protect coastal wetlands.","The area of Merrow National Park (6,494 square kilometers) is greater than that of Brookhaven National Park (4,226 square kilometers)."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.1d645325bb","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-396","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lake Sable is in southern Norway. It was first surveyed in 1904.</li><li>Its surface area is 663 square kilometers.</li><li>Lake Valewood is in northern India. It was first surveyed in 2007.</li><li>Its surface area is 313 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are reservoirs created by dams.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between Lake Sable and Lake Valewood.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The surface area of Lake Sable (663 square kilometers) is greater than that of Lake Valewood (313 square kilometers).","Lake Sable was first surveyed in 1904, while Lake Valewood was first surveyed in 2007.","Both Lake Sable and Lake Valewood are reservoirs created by dams.","Lake Sable is in southern Norway, and Lake Valewood is in northern India."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.05724de8da","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-397","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Umber Museum is in central Spain. It opened in 1929.</li><li>Its annual attendance is 319,340 visitors.</li><li>The Halden Museum is in eastern Australia. It opened in 1918.</li><li>Its annual attendance is 309,707 visitors.</li><li>Both are museums of natural history.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast the locations of the two museums.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The annual attendance of the Umber Museum (319,340 visitors) is greater than that of the Halden Museum (309,707 visitors).","The Umber Museum opened in 1929, while the Halden Museum opened in 1918.","The Umber Museum is in central Spain, and the Halden Museum is in eastern Australia.","The Umber Museum, which is in central Spain, is one of several museums of natural history."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It names where both are and contrasts them. The other choices compare dates or size, or mention only one location."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.24f9707a98","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-8","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lucía Sato is an ecologist.</li><li>Sato wanted to find out whether city lights change when songbirds begin singing in the morning.</li><li>Sato compared the first songs of robins in brightly lit city parks with those in dark rural woodlands.</li><li>Sato found that robins in lit parks began singing nearly an hour earlier.</li><li>Sato concluded that artificial light may shift birds' daily rhythms.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Sato concluded that artificial light may shift birds' daily rhythms.","Sato found that robins in lit parks began singing nearly an hour earlier.","Because robins in lit parks began singing nearly an hour earlier, Sato concluded that artificial light may shift birds' daily rhythms.","To investigate the question, Sato compared the first songs of robins in brightly lit city parks with those in dark rural woodlands."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.1b440ae64c","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-25","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Elias Takeda is a materials scientist.</li><li>Takeda wanted to find out whether a coating made from seaweed can keep fruit fresh longer.</li><li>Takeda coated half of a batch of strawberries with a seaweed-based film and left the rest uncoated.</li><li>Takeda found that coated strawberries stayed fresh for about four days longer.</li><li>Takeda concluded that the coating could help reduce food waste.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Takeda coated half of a batch of strawberries with a seaweed-based film and left the rest uncoated.","Takeda found that coated strawberries stayed fresh for about four days longer.","Materials scientist Elias Takeda set out to learn whether a coating made from seaweed can keep fruit fresh longer.","Takeda concluded that the coating could help reduce food waste."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.210ab75ed5","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-58","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Diego Iyer is a zoologist.</li><li>Iyer wanted to find out whether zoo elephants that are given puzzle feeders are more active.</li><li>Iyer gave puzzle feeders to some elephants and standard food trays to others, then tracked their movement.</li><li>Iyer found that elephants with puzzle feeders walked noticeably more each day.</li><li>Iyer concluded that puzzle feeders may encourage healthy activity.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Iyer gave puzzle feeders to some elephants and standard food trays to others, then tracked their movement.","Iyer found that elephants with puzzle feeders walked noticeably more each day.","Iyer concluded that puzzle feeders may encourage healthy activity.","Because elephants with puzzle feeders walked noticeably more each day, Iyer concluded that puzzle feeders may encourage healthy activity."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.a2a62d9869","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-65","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Leila Castellano is an astronomer.</li><li>Castellano wanted to find out whether a distant star's brightness changes on a regular schedule.</li><li>Castellano measured the star's brightness every night for eighteen months.</li><li>Castellano found that the star dimmed slightly about every 42 days.</li><li>Castellano concluded that an orbiting planet may be passing in front of the star.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Castellano measured the star's brightness every night for eighteen months.","Astronomer Leila Castellano set out to learn whether a distant star's brightness changes on a regular schedule.","Castellano found that the star dimmed slightly about every 42 days.","Castellano concluded that an orbiting planet may be passing in front of the star."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.10b1a72976","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-73","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Dmitri Vance is an economist.</li><li>Vance wanted to find out whether a four-day workweek changes employee productivity.</li><li>Vance tracked output at companies before and after they adopted a four-day week.</li><li>Vance found that output per employee stayed the same or rose slightly.</li><li>Vance concluded that shorter workweeks may not reduce productivity.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Economist Dmitri Vance set out to learn whether a four-day workweek changes employee productivity.","Vance concluded that shorter workweeks may not reduce productivity.","Vance found that output per employee stayed the same or rose slightly.","To investigate the question, Vance tracked output at companies before and after they adopted a four-day week."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.73e47fb443","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-74","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Felipe Moreau is an economist.</li><li>Moreau wanted to find out whether a four-day workweek changes employee productivity.</li><li>Moreau tracked output at companies before and after they adopted a four-day week.</li><li>Moreau found that output per employee stayed the same or rose slightly.</li><li>Moreau concluded that shorter workweeks may not reduce productivity.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Because output per employee stayed the same or rose slightly, Moreau concluded that shorter workweeks may not reduce productivity.","Moreau found that output per employee stayed the same or rose slightly.","Moreau concluded that shorter workweeks may not reduce productivity.","To investigate the question, Moreau tracked output at companies before and after they adopted a four-day week."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.a70eac990f","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-75","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Tomás Varga is an economist.</li><li>Varga wanted to find out whether a four-day workweek changes employee productivity.</li><li>Varga tracked output at companies before and after they adopted a four-day week.</li><li>Varga found that output per employee stayed the same or rose slightly.</li><li>Varga concluded that shorter workweeks may not reduce productivity.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Varga concluded that shorter workweeks may not reduce productivity.","To investigate the question, Varga tracked output at companies before and after they adopted a four-day week.","Because output per employee stayed the same or rose slightly, Varga concluded that shorter workweeks may not reduce productivity.","Varga found that output per employee stayed the same or rose slightly."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.db60a749e9","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-76","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Beatriz Rahman is an economist.</li><li>Rahman wanted to find out whether a four-day workweek changes employee productivity.</li><li>Rahman tracked output at companies before and after they adopted a four-day week.</li><li>Rahman found that output per employee stayed the same or rose slightly.</li><li>Rahman concluded that shorter workweeks may not reduce productivity.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Rahman concluded that shorter workweeks may not reduce productivity.","To investigate the question, Rahman tracked output at companies before and after they adopted a four-day week.","Rahman found that output per employee stayed the same or rose slightly.","Economist Beatriz Rahman set out to learn whether a four-day workweek changes employee productivity."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.bbc2fd4938","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-88","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Elias Moreau is an urban planner.</li><li>Moreau wanted to find out whether adding trees along streets lowers summer temperatures.</li><li>Moreau measured afternoon temperatures on streets with and without tree cover.</li><li>Moreau found that streets with trees were up to four degrees cooler.</li><li>Moreau concluded that street trees may help cities stay cooler in summer.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Moreau found that streets with trees were up to four degrees cooler.","Moreau concluded that street trees may help cities stay cooler in summer.","Because streets with trees were up to four degrees cooler, Moreau concluded that street trees may help cities stay cooler in summer.","To investigate the question, Moreau measured afternoon temperatures on streets with and without tree cover."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.57ab259be3","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-107","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Diego Halvorsen is a chemist.</li><li>Halvorsen wanted to find out whether a new plant-based plastic breaks down in seawater.</li><li>Halvorsen placed samples of the plastic in tanks of seawater for six months.</li><li>Halvorsen found that most samples broke apart within four months.</li><li>Halvorsen concluded that the plastic may be less harmful to oceans than conventional plastic.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Halvorsen found that most samples broke apart within four months.","Because most samples broke apart within four months, Halvorsen concluded that the plastic may be less harmful to oceans than conventional plastic.","To investigate the question, Halvorsen placed samples of the plastic in tanks of seawater for six months.","Halvorsen concluded that the plastic may be less harmful to oceans than conventional plastic."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.fa1b8deaef","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-112","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Samuel Adeyemi is a primatologist.</li><li>Adeyemi wanted to find out whether young chimpanzees learn to use tools by watching adults.</li><li>Adeyemi observed young chimpanzees in groups where adults cracked nuts with stones and in groups where adults did not.</li><li>Adeyemi found that young chimpanzees in the nut-cracking groups began using stones much earlier.</li><li>Adeyemi concluded that young chimpanzees may learn tool use by observation.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Adeyemi concluded that young chimpanzees may learn tool use by observation.","Primatologist Samuel Adeyemi set out to learn whether young chimpanzees learn to use tools by watching adults.","Adeyemi found that young chimpanzees in the nut-cracking groups began using stones much earlier.","To investigate the question, Adeyemi observed young chimpanzees in groups where adults cracked nuts with stones and in groups where adults did not."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.12634f21f7","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-114","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Beatriz Mensah is a sleep researcher.</li><li>Mensah wanted to find out whether using screens before bed affects how quickly people fall asleep.</li><li>Mensah asked volunteers to read either a printed book or a tablet for an hour before bed.</li><li>Mensah found that tablet readers took about ten minutes longer to fall asleep.</li><li>Mensah concluded that screen use before bed may delay sleep.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Mensah asked volunteers to read either a printed book or a tablet for an hour before bed.","Sleep researcher Beatriz Mensah set out to learn whether using screens before bed affects how quickly people fall asleep.","Mensah found that tablet readers took about ten minutes longer to fall asleep.","Mensah concluded that screen use before bed may delay sleep."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.397bbfb0cf","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-127","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kofi Duarte is a public health researcher.</li><li>Duarte wanted to find out whether adding bike lanes increases how often residents cycle.</li><li>Duarte counted cyclists on streets before and after new bike lanes were added.</li><li>Duarte found that cycling on those streets nearly doubled within a year.</li><li>Duarte concluded that bike lanes may encourage more people to ride.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Public health researcher Kofi Duarte set out to learn whether adding bike lanes increases how often residents cycle.","To investigate the question, Duarte counted cyclists on streets before and after new bike lanes were added.","Duarte found that cycling on those streets nearly doubled within a year.","Duarte concluded that bike lanes may encourage more people to ride."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.e78da248c1","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-142","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Zara Sato is a neuroscientist.</li><li>Sato wanted to find out whether learning to juggle changes brain structure.</li><li>Sato scanned volunteers' brains before and after three months of juggling practice.</li><li>Sato found that regions linked to tracking movement grew slightly larger.</li><li>Sato concluded that practicing a new skill may reshape parts of the brain.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Neuroscientist Zara Sato set out to learn whether learning to juggle changes brain structure.","Sato found that regions linked to tracking movement grew slightly larger.","To investigate the question, Sato scanned volunteers' brains before and after three months of juggling practice.","Sato concluded that practicing a new skill may reshape parts of the brain."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.b5fc1a1ac8","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-145","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Priya Takeda is a marine ecologist.</li><li>Takeda wanted to find out whether sea urchins limit the growth of kelp forests.</li><li>Takeda fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open.</li><li>Takeda found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser.</li><li>Takeda concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Takeda fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open.","Takeda concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.","Takeda found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser.","Because kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser, Takeda concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.6b4e63aea6","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-154","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ingrid Mensah is a climate scientist.</li><li>Mensah wanted to find out whether light-colored roofs reduce indoor temperatures.</li><li>Mensah compared indoor temperatures in homes with white roofs and homes with dark roofs.</li><li>Mensah found that homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler.</li><li>Mensah concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Climate scientist Ingrid Mensah set out to learn whether light-colored roofs reduce indoor temperatures.","To investigate the question, Mensah compared indoor temperatures in homes with white roofs and homes with dark roofs.","Mensah found that homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler.","Mensah concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.98bd527f1f","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-174","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Sofia Okafor is a music researcher.</li><li>Okafor wanted to find out whether learning an instrument improves children's listening skills.</li><li>Okafor tested children's ability to pick out sounds in noise before and after two years of music lessons.</li><li>Okafor found that children who took lessons improved more than those who did not.</li><li>Okafor concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Okafor found that children who took lessons improved more than those who did not.","Okafor concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills.","To investigate the question, Okafor tested children's ability to pick out sounds in noise before and after two years of music lessons.","Because children who took lessons improved more than those who did not, Okafor concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.f97070fa01","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-179","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Beatriz Delacroix is a music researcher.</li><li>Delacroix wanted to find out whether learning an instrument improves children's listening skills.</li><li>Delacroix tested children's ability to pick out sounds in noise before and after two years of music lessons.</li><li>Delacroix found that children who took lessons improved more than those who did not.</li><li>Delacroix concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Because children who took lessons improved more than those who did not, Delacroix concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills.","Delacroix concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills.","To investigate the question, Delacroix tested children's ability to pick out sounds in noise before and after two years of music lessons.","Delacroix found that children who took lessons improved more than those who did not."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."}
